{{also|bóp|bớp|вор|BOP|Bop}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/bɒp/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɒp|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
Imitative of the sound made.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|colloquial|onomatopoeia}} A very light [[smack]], [[blow]] or [[punch]].

====Verb====
{{en-verb|bops|bopping|bopped}}

# {{lb|en|colloquial|transitive}} To [[strike]] gently or playfully.
#* {{quote-text|year=2013|author=Karin Tanabe|title=The List|page=37|passage=“Better him than me,” I said while my mother fluttered her blue eyes at me and '''bopped''' me on the nose with a wooden spoon.}}

=====Synonyms=====
* [[bip]], [[boop]]

===Etymology 2===
Shortened from [[bebop]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[style]] of [[improvised]] [[jazz]] from the 1940s.
# {{lb|en|countable|UK|Oxbridge slang}} A [[party]].
#* {{quote-text|year=2005|author=Johnny Rich|title=Push Guide to Which University|page=472|passage=Theatres; Music House used for bands; May Ball; very popular weekly '''bops''' in JCR and MCR; library (57,000 books); 40 networked PCs, 24-hrs.}}
#* {{quote-text|year=2012|author=Owen Jones|title=Chavs: The Demonization of the Working Class|page=120|passage=At universities like Oxford, middle-class students hold 'chav '''bops'''' where they dress up as this working-class caricature.}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|bops|bopping|bopped}}

# To [[dance]] to this [[music]], or any sort of popular music with a strong beat.

